    # 14 — p104 tips: radiator balancing basics
    dict(
        pillar="tips",
        topic="Why one radiator is always cold — balancing basics",
        slug="radiators", suffix="radiator-balancing",
        hint="asset:radiator", itype="asset", banner="upper",
        caption="Why one radiator's always cold 🥶",
        cta=None, extra_cta="Save this for the first cold week of autumn.",
        fb="🥶 One radiator roasting, another barely warm? That's not a broken rad — it's an unbalanced system.\n\nHot water is lazy. It takes the easiest route back to the boiler, so the radiators nearest it hog the flow and the far ones get leftovers.\n\nBalancing fixes it: the lockshield valve (the capped one) gets closed down slightly on the greedy rads, pushing flow out to the lazy ones.\n\n⚠️ If a rad is cold at the BOTTOM though, that's sludge, not balance — different problem, different fix.",
        ig="One radiator roasting, another barely warm? 🥶\n\nThat's not a broken radiator — it's an unbalanced system.\n\nHot water is lazy: it takes the shortest route back to the boiler. The rads nearest it hog the flow, the far ones get leftovers.\n\nThe fix is balancing — closing the lockshield valve (the capped one) slightly on the greedy radiators to push flow to the lazy ones.\n\n⚠️ Cold at the BOTTOM is different — that's sludge, not balance.",
        tw="One rad roasting, another barely warm? That's balance, not a fault. 🥶 Closing the lockshield on greedy rads pushes flow to the lazy ones.\n\nCold at the BOTTOM = sludge. Different fix.",
        gb="If one radiator in your home roasts while another barely warms up, the radiator itself is rarely the problem — the system is out of balance. Heated water naturally takes the easiest route back to the boiler, so radiators closest to it take more than their share of the flow while distant ones receive leftovers. Balancing corrects this: the lockshield valve on each radiator is adjusted so flow is distributed evenly around the house. It's a methodical job with a thermometer rather than guesswork. Note that a radiator cold at the bottom is a different issue — that points to sludge build-up, which balancing won't cure. Radiator work across Southampton.",
        tags=["#RadiatorTips", "#CentralHeating", "#HeatingTips", "#HomeTips"],
    ),
