    # 16 — p106 cost_reveal: hard water limescale cost in Southampton
    dict(
        pillar="cost_reveal",
        topic="What Southampton's hard water quietly costs your boiler",
        slug="boiler-service", suffix="hard-water",
        hint="ai", itype="ai", banner="lower",
        caption="Hard water is taxing your boiler 💧",
        cta="SERVICE", extra_cta=None,
        fb="💧 Southampton sits on hard water — and your kettle has been trying to tell you for years.\n\nThat same crusty scale forms inside your boiler's heat exchanger. Scale is an insulator: the thicker it gets, the harder the boiler works to push the same heat into your water. You pay for that extra work on every single gas bill.\n\nLeft long enough, it 'kettles' — that rumbling, boiling-pot noise — and parts start failing early.\n\nAn annual service catches scale problems early, and the right water treatment slows the build-up right down.",
        ig="Look inside your kettle. Your boiler looks the same. 💧\n\nSouthampton is a hard-water city — and the scale crusting your kettle is also forming inside your boiler's heat exchanger.\n\nScale insulates. The thicker it gets, the harder the boiler works for the same heat — and you pay the difference on every gas bill.\n\nLeft alone it 'kettles' (that rumbling boiling-pot noise) and parts fail early.\n\nAn annual service + the right water treatment slows it right down.",
        tw="Your kettle's limescale? It's inside your boiler too. 💧\n\nHard water coats the heat exchanger — the boiler works harder, you pay more, parts fail early. A service catches it early.",
        gb="Southampton is a hard-water area, and the limescale you see in your kettle is also forming where you can't see it — inside your boiler's heat exchanger. Scale acts as an insulating layer, so the boiler has to work progressively harder to transfer the same heat into your water, and that extra work shows up on every gas bill. Left unchecked it leads to 'kettling', the rumbling noise of water boiling against scaled metal, and to premature component failure. An annual service identifies scale issues early, and appropriate water treatment — inhibitor and a scale reducer where suitable — slows the build-up considerably. Worth addressing before winter.",
        tags=["#HardWater", "#Limescale", "#BoilerService", "#GasBill"],
        prompt="SCENE: a bright UK kitchen worktop, natural window light, photorealistic close-up.\nSUBJECT: an open stainless kettle showing heavy white limescale crusted on its heating element, a glass of clear water beside it. Text on a clean navy banner across the lower third reads (EXACT TEXT, verbatim, no substitutions, no duplicates): \"Hard water is taxing your boiler 💧\"\nDETAILS: navy #0A2540 banner with a thin orange #FF6B00 accent bar, white bold sans-serif type; cool clean palette, no people, no brand logos.\nUSE CASE: Instagram social post, square 1:1, 1080x1080.\nCONSTRAINTS: Render text verbatim, no extra words, no duplicate lines, no fake logos. Photorealistic, not illustrative.",
    ),
    # 17 — p107 emergency: frozen condensate quick fix
    dict(
        pillar="emergency",
        topic="Frozen condensate pipe — the quick fix walkthrough",
        slug="boiler-repair", suffix="frozen-condensate-fix",
        hint="ai", itype="ai", banner="upper",
        caption="Frozen condensate? Try this first ❄️",
        cta=None, extra_cta="Save this for the first hard frost.",
        fb="❄️ First hard frost of winter, boiler's dead, and there's a gurgling noise? Nine times out of ten it's a frozen condensate pipe — and you can often fix it yourself in ten minutes.\n\n1️⃣ Find the white plastic pipe running from the boiler to an outside drain\n2️⃣ Pour WARM water along it — never boiling, it can crack the pipe\n3️⃣ A hot water bottle held on the frozen section works too\n4️⃣ Once thawed, reset the boiler\n\nIf it fires up — sorted. If it locks out again, there's more going on and it needs a proper look.",
        ig="The 10-minute fix for a frosty-morning boiler death ❄️\n\nFirst hard frost, no heating, gurgling noise from the boiler? It's almost always a frozen condensate pipe.\n\n1️⃣ Find the white plastic pipe running outside from the boiler\n2️⃣ Pour WARM water along it — never boiling (it can crack the pipe)\n3️⃣ Hot water bottle on the frozen bit works too\n4️⃣ Reset the boiler once thawed\n\nFires up? Sorted. Locks out again? Something more is going on — get it looked at.",
        tw="Boiler dead on a frosty morning + gurgling = frozen condensate pipe ❄️\n\nPour WARM (never boiling) water along the white outside pipe, then reset. Locks out again? Get it looked at.",
        gb="On the first hard frost of winter, one fault accounts for a huge share of 'dead boiler' calls: the frozen condensate pipe. It's the white plastic pipe that runs from your boiler to an outside drain, and when the trapped water inside freezes, the boiler shuts itself down — often with a gurgling sound and a fault code. The fix is frequently something you can do yourself: pour warm (never boiling) water along the frozen section, or hold a hot water bottle against it, then reset the boiler. If it fires up, you're sorted; if it locks out again, there's an underlying issue worth a professional look. Boiler repairs across Southampton.",
        tags=["#BoilerTips", "#WinterTips", "#FrozenPipe", "#BoilerRepair"],
        prompt="SCENE: a UK house exterior brick wall on a frosty winter morning, low golden light, visible frost on surfaces, photorealistic.\nSUBJECT: a white plastic condensate pipe running down the brick wall to a drain, a jug of warm water being poured along the frosted pipe by a gloved hand entering frame from the side. Text on a clean navy banner across the upper third reads (EXACT TEXT, verbatim, no substitutions, no duplicates): \"Frozen condensate? Try this first ❄️\"\nDETAILS: navy #0A2540 banner with a thin orange #FF6B00 accent bar, white bold sans-serif type; cold blue-and-brick palette, gentle steam rising from the warm water.\nUSE CASE: Instagram social post, square 1:1, 1080x1080.\nCONSTRAINTS: Render text verbatim, no extra words, no duplicate lines, no fake logos. Photorealistic, not illustrative. Only one hand and jug visible, no face, no extra pipes or fittings.",
    ),
